Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Anton,

I don´t know if you are still struggling with this question, but I seem to have found a better solution to reading csv files.  For method read.csv you can pass a parameter called 'colClasses' as follows:

read.csv("../test/reputation.csv", sep = "#", header = false, colClasses: c("character", "numeric", "numeric", "character", "character",
      "numeric"))

In this case, I´m saying that the first column should be read as character, the second as numeric, etc.  My reputation.csv file has an IP address on the first colum of the form "254.123.65.11" and when read by read.csv without the colClasses param, the first column only has 254.123 as if it were a number.  With colClasses and 'character' the whole IP is read.

Hope this helps.

Cheers,

Em terça-feira, 17 de março de 2015 17:26:15 UTC-3, Rodrigo Botafogo escreveu:
> Anton,
>
> The code bellow is not R, but SciCom.  SciCom is a wrapper to Renjin for Ruby (so one does not need to compile java code).  If you are calling Renjin from java, then just write normal R code.  I´m not an expert in R, but I would say that in R you should do
>
> data0 <- data0[-1,]     (yes nothing after the ',')
> data0$names = c("id", "operations", ....)
>
> Now I think there might to be a better way.  I think that read.csv bug is that when it reads the date on your cvs file as 2015-03-01, it assumes that this is actually a number and not a string, and thus stops scanning the string at 2015, since '-' is not part of a number.  If you can change your csv file so that dates start with a character, for instance, suppose you have your date as mar-03-2015, your R scripts might actually work.  I haven´t tested it.
>
> Good luck,
>
>
>
> Em domingo, 15 de março de 2015 11:10:49 UTC-3, Anton escreveu:
